// **Foliage that came off with its branch, lying where the branch landed.**
//
// The owner's ruling, watching a fell (card `20260829T034016675Z-5a9b07`):
// *"The leaves shouldn't turn to powder ever. They can stay on the branch if
// that is easier (but if they do fall off, i feel like they should stay as
// larger leaf pieces). It should look like a bunch of branches are broken on
// the ground."*
//
// Before this, `leaf.ron`'s `severs_into` was `litter` — a `Powder` with a
// friction angle — so the instant a severed limb touched down, every leaf on
// it became a grain and ran off into a cone. Measured on `scene=fell
// fell=7150`: `litter` 992 cells and `deadwood` 557 against `log` 868, i.e.
// **the settled pile was two-thirds loose grain**, and `open-bugs-handoff.md`
// T1f records that this, not the fragment ladder, is what has failed the
// acceptance twice. 91%+ of *woody* mass already promotes as coherent pieces;
// what the eye sees is the foliage lying over the top of them.
//
// So this is the **third** debris tier, and it is to `leaf` exactly what
// `log` is to `wood`: the piece, as against the grit. `litter` is not
// replaced and does not move — it is still what a *shed* leaf becomes, still
// what the ant, decay and fire layers read, and still what this rots down
// into. The difference is that a leaf now reaches it by **decaying**, over
// time, rather than by being pulverised the moment its branch stops moving.
// That is `CLAUDE.md`'s first law arriving where it was missing: the outcome
// has a middle.
//
// # Why a Powder that cannot flow, and not a Solid — measured, both ways
//
// `Solid` is the obvious reading of "leaves should not turn to powder", it
// is what `log` does for the wood tier, and **it was built and rendered and
// it is wrong**. A severed crown converted to Solid in mid-air; the first
// pieces to land became a scaffold that nothing could ever fail, because a
// piece tier is opted out of the structural check; and the tree **died
// standing in the shape it grew in** instead of falling. `scene=fell
// fell=7150`, unmistakable at 3x.
//
// Giving it the lowest real span instead (`max_unsupported_span: 2`) fails
// the other way and just as hard: the crown comes apart on the way down and
// the settled box goes to **80% loose grain against a 76% baseline**, with
// `deadwood` 557 -> 941 as the failing foliage takes the logs' support with
// it. Two settings of one knob, opposite failures — `CLAUDE.md`'s own signal
// that the knob is not the problem.
//
// **The finding underneath is worth more than either attempt, and it
// reframes the whole package**: what has always *looked* like a crown
// collapsing was **powder flowing downhill**, not a fall. Nothing in the
// engine yet makes a severed piece travel. Take the grain away and there is
// no fall left at all — which is why the Solid version leaves a standing
// dead tree, and why this material cannot be judged on its own until a
// piece genuinely falls over (`Reports/tree-mechanics-plan-2026-08-29.md`
// §7 stage 4).
//
// So: a Powder, which falls, at a friction angle steep enough that it does
// not *flow*. That separates the two properties the Solid attempt conflated
// — coming down is not the same as running out — and it is a knob with a
// measured floor (`litter` at 42 already mats rather than slumping) rather
// than a binary.
//
// `anchors_organisms: false` for the reason `log` carries it: a leafy limb
// landing beside a stump must not hold up the crown the cut just detached.
// Measured when `log` first shipped, that took `acceptance.sh`'s `fell` case
// from 2,360 cells severed to **0**.
(
    name: "deadleaf",
    display: "Dead foliage",
    kind: Powder,
    // **85 degrees, against `litter`'s 42 and sand's 34 — the steepest in
    // the shipped set, and this is the whole mechanism.** A clump of dead
    // foliage falls, because anything that does not fall becomes a scaffold
    // (see the Solid attempt above); but it must not *flow*, because
    // flowing to an angle of repose is the cone of dust the owner rejected.
    // At 85 a drift holds a near-vertical face, so a limb's worth of leaves
    // comes down and stays in the shape it landed in instead of running out
    // across the floor.
    friction_angle: 85.0,
    // `leaf` is 0.25 and `litter` 0.3. Foliage does not get heavier by
    // dying, and the number matters: it is what the moment model weighs a
    // crown by, and it is why 48% of a tree's cells are only 20% of its
    // mass.
    density: 0.25,
    // **`leaf.ron`'s palette, copied entry for entry, and that is the whole
    // point of this block.** Owner, third time of asking: *"The color should
    // not change at all (not sure how many times I have to say this) and
    // most of the leaves should stay on the branch."*
    //
    // The first two readings of that were wrong and both are worth recording
    // because they were plausible. It is not the *log* changing from bark to
    // pale timber (fixed, `3bdf674`), and it is not the day/night cycle
    // moving the ambient light between tiles of a contact sheet (a card I
    // built badly, and the owner had to say so). **It is the leaves.** A
    // living leaf is green; the first draft of this material invented an
    // olive-khaki "dead foliage" tone, so cutting a branch turned its green
    // leaves brown the instant they landed. Nobody asked for the foliage to
    // look dead -- what was asked for is that it stay on the branch.
    //
    // A freshly cut limb has green leaves on it for days. So this palette is
    // `leaf`'s, index for index, and `rigid::settle` carries the cell's own
    // shade across rather than re-rolling it (it does that whenever the two
    // palettes are the same length -- see `settled_shade`'s call site). The
    // colour is therefore **bit-identical** before and after the tier change,
    // which is the only reading of "should not change at all" that cannot be
    // got wrong a fourth time.
    //
    // Keeping all eight bands matters and is not padding: a band is a
    // *species*' leaf colour, picked by its own range, so copying the whole
    // thing is what makes a felled conifer stay conifer-green and a felled
    // birch stay birch-green. Four colours here would have flattened every
    // species to one.
    //
    // What says "this is dead" is not the hue, it is `decays_into` below:
    // the foliage browns off into `litter` over time, on a schedule, instead
    // of at the instant of the cut.
    colors: [
        // 0: deep blue-green — fir, spruce
        (42, 74, 52),
        (52, 90, 62),
        (34, 62, 44),
        (60, 102, 72),
        // 1: mid blue-green — pine
        (46, 86, 58),
        (56, 102, 70),
        (38, 72, 48),
        (66, 116, 80),
        // 2: mid green — the original palette
        (58, 96, 40),
        (70, 116, 48),
        (48, 82, 34),
        (82, 132, 56),
        // 3: yellow-green — birch, willow
        (86, 118, 44),
        (102, 138, 54),
        (72, 100, 36),
        (118, 158, 66),
        // 4: olive — dry-site shrubs
        (94, 104, 56),
        (110, 122, 68),
        (78, 88, 46),
        (126, 140, 80),
        // 5: dark olive
        (66, 76, 38),
        (80, 92, 46),
        (54, 62, 30),
        (94, 108, 56),
        // 6: pale sage — glaucous, ivy and the ground layer
        (104, 126, 100),
        (120, 144, 116),
        (86, 106, 82),
        (138, 162, 134),
        // 7: deep sage
        (76, 98, 76),
        (90, 114, 90),
        (62, 82, 62),
        (106, 132, 106),
    ],
    // Drier than the living leaf it was (0.75, burns out in 40) and drier
    // than `litter` (0.5), because this is foliage that has died on the
    // branch rather than foliage lying on damp ground. A felled crown left
    // to stand is the most flammable thing in a wood, which is a real
    // property worth having and not a number invented to be interesting.
    flammability: 0.7,
    ignition_temperature: 320.0,
    burn_temperature: 620.0,
    burn_duration: 60,
    burns_into: "ash",
    heat_conductivity: 0.1,
    // **The grit tier, reached two ways.** Hit it, blast it or burn round it
    // and it comes apart into `litter` at once; leave it alone and
    // `decays_into` takes it there slowly. Same material either way, which
    // is what keeps the ant and fire layers unchanged — they read `litter`
    // and `litter` is still what a forest floor is made of.
    breaks_into: "litter",
    anchors_organisms: false,
    // **Rots into the forest floor, and this is the rate that decides
    // whether the mechanic reads.** Too fast and a felled crown is a gold
    // drift again inside a few hundred frames, which is the state this
    // material exists to remove. Too slow and a wood fills with khaki that
    // never becomes ground.
    //
    // Set between `litter` (0.5 damp / 0.1 dry — a shed leaf is thin, wet
    // and already on the soil) and `log` (0.02 / 0.002 — a bole is not).
    // Foliage on a fallen branch is off the ground and still whole, so it
    // is nearer the log end than the leaf end. **A number to sweep, not a
    // derived one**, and the quantity to sweep it against is how long a
    // felled crown reads as a felled crown.
    decays_into: "litter",
    decay_chance_damp: 0.08,
    decay_chance_dry: 0.015,
    // Unchanged from `litter`, so a stand's felled foliage is worth to an
    // ant exactly what its shed foliage was. Moving it would be an ecology
    // change riding along with a rendering one, which is the kind of thing
    // `CLAUDE.md` records costing a session to attribute afterwards.
    food_energy: 480.0,
    food_class: -1.0,
)
